= Solution
{parent=i}

The <hidden subgroup problem> is to recover a <subgroup> $K\leq G$, usually by giving <generators of a group>, from an <oracle machine> providing $f:G\to Y$ with the promise
$$
\boxed{f(g)=f(h)\iff gK=hK.}
$$
Thus $f$ is constant on each left <coset> and distinguishes different left <cosets>. The input includes an effective representation of elements of the <group> and access to the group operations; the desired efficiency is measured against the number of bits describing an element, rather than necessarily against $|G|$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write the <finite abelian group> additively. Its <group shift operator> is
$$
\boxed{U(h)|g\rangle=|g+h\rangle.}
$$
These <unitary operators> form the <regular representation> and commute. The representation-theoretic facts we use are that every <irreducible representation of a finite abelian group> is one-dimensional, there are $|G|$ such <characters of a representation>, and their <character orthogonality> gives an <orthonormal basis> of functions on $G$. Each <linear character> here is a <group homomorphism> $\chi:G\to U(1)$, with $|\chi(g)|=1$.

For $\chi\in\widehat G$, the <character group of a finite abelian group>, put
$$
|v_\chi\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt{|G|}}\sum_{g\in G}\overline{\chi(g)}|g\rangle.
$$
Changing variables to $u=g+h$ gives
$$
U(h)|v_\chi\rangle
=\frac1{\sqrt{|G|}}\sum_u\overline{\chi(u-h)}|u\rangle
=\boxed{\chi(h)|v_\chi\rangle}.
$$
The $|G|$ vectors $|v_\chi\rangle$ are therefore a common <eigenbasis>. Using $\chi(g)$ instead of its <complex conjugate> in their definition reverses every <eigenphase>; this is merely the <quantum Fourier transform> sign convention.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Measure the <coset state> in the common <eigenbasis> $\{|v_\chi\rangle\}$ of the <group shift operators>. Its overlap is
$$
\langle v_\chi|g_0+K\rangle
=\frac{\chi(g_0)}{\sqrt{|G||K|}}\sum_{k\in K}\chi(k).
$$
The <character-sum cancellation lemma> makes this sum $|K|$ when $\chi$ is trivial on $K$, and zero otherwise. Hence, with $K^\perp$ the <annihilator of a subgroup of a finite abelian group>,
$$
\boxed{\Pr(\chi)=\begin{cases}|K|/|G|,&\chi\in K^\perp,\\0,&\chi\notin K^\perp.\end{cases}}
$$
Since $|K^\perp|=|G|/|K|$, this is the <uniform distribution on a finite set> $K^\perp$. The factor $\chi(g_0)$ has <modulus> one, so the distribution is independent of $g_0$. This is <abelian hidden-subgroup Fourier sampling>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The distinct values in one period imply that $r$ is the least positive period, $r\mid N$, and $|Y|=r$. Write $x=j+tr$, where $0\leq j<r$ and $0\leq t<N/r$. Use the positive-exponent <quantum Fourier transform> convention already used in the concept article. The amplitude of first-register value $y$ is
$$
\frac1N\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}e^{2\pi ijy/N}|f(j)\rangle
\sum_{t=0}^{N/r-1}e^{2\pi itry/N}.
$$
By the <root-of-unity filter>, the inner sum vanishes unless $y=mN/r$. Define
$$
\boxed{M=\{mN/r:0\leq m<r\},\qquad
|e_{mN/r}\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt r}\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}e^{2\pi imj/r}|f(j)\rangle.}
$$
The distinct $f(j)$ give an <orthonormal basis> of the second register, and the transformed state is
$$
|\xi\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt r}\sum_{m=0}^{r-1}|mN/r\rangle|e_{mN/r}\rangle.
$$
Reindexing $j+l$ modulo $r$ shows
$$
\boxed{U_l|e_{mN/r}\rangle=e^{-2\pi iml/r}|e_{mN/r}\rangle.}
$$
Consequently measurement in this common <eigenbasis> returns each $m$ with probability $1/r$; the corresponding <eigenvalue> of $U_1$ is a uniformly sampled $r$th <root of unity>. A single measured phase $-m/r$ reveals the denominator $r/\gcd(m,r)$, which need not be $r$. Repeated samples permit <exact period recovery from a Fourier sample>, for example by taking the <least common multiple> of their reduced denominators. The simultaneous eigenspaces and their probabilities depend on $r$, not on the particular labels $f(j)$.

