= Solution
{parent=i}

The <spectral norm> is the <operator norm> induced by the <Euclidean norm>:
$$
\|A\|=\sup_{v\ne0}\frac{\|Av\|_2}{\|v\|_2}
=\sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(A^\dagger A)}.
$$
The <Pauli operators> $X_1$ and $Z_2$ act on different <qubits>, and each is a <unitary operator>. Their product is therefore unitary and preserves the norm of every vector. Hence
$$
\boxed{\|X_1Z_2\|=1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For finite-dimensional <matrices> $A_1,\ldots,A_m$, the <Lie-Trotter product formula> states
$$
\boxed{e^{t\sum_{j=1}^m A_j}
=\lim_{k\to\infty}\left(e^{tA_1/k}\cdots e^{tA_m/k}\right)^k,}
$$
where convergence is in <operator norm>. In particular, for <Hermitian matrices> $H_j$, set $A_j=-iH_j$ to obtain a product of <unitary operators> approximating $e^{-it\sum_jH_j}$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The printed sum contains $Z_n$ although the stated labels end at $n-1$. We use the natural periodic convention $Z_n=Z_0$, with $n\geq3$. If an open chain was intended, omitting the final term gives the same asymptotic bound.

Set $H_j=X_{j-1}Z_j$, with indices modulo $n$, and implement the <product-formula Hamiltonian simulation>
$$
\widetilde U=\left(\prod_{j=1}^n e^{-iH_j/k}\right)^k.
$$
Each factor acts on two <qubits>, so it is a two-qubit <unitary operator>. More explicitly, if $a=j-1$, $b=j$, and $C_{a\to b}$ is a <controlled-NOT gate>,
$$
e^{-i\delta X_aZ_b}
=\mathsf H_a C_{a\to b}\,e^{-i\delta Z_b}\,C_{a\to b}\mathsf H_a,
$$
where $\mathsf H_a$ is a <Hadamard gate>. This gives a constant number of one-qubit and two-qubit gates per factor; one-qubit gates can also be viewed as two-qubit gates tensored with the identity.

The <spectral norm> obeys the <triangle inequality>, <submultiplicativity of the operator norm>, and invariance under multiplication by <unitary operators>. In particular, the <telescoping bound for products of operators> gives $\|V^k-W^k\|\leq k\|V-W\|$ for unitary $V,W$. The <first-order unitary product-formula error bound> consequently yields
$$
\|U-\widetilde U\|\leq\frac1{2k}\sum_{j<l}\|[H_j,H_l]\|.
$$
Only neighboring terms can have a nonzero <commutator>, because all other supports are disjoint. There are $n$ neighboring unordered pairs, and $\|[H_j,H_l]\|\leq2\|H_j\|\|H_l\|=2$. Thus $\|U-\widetilde U\|\leq n/k$. Choosing $k>n/\varepsilon$ gives
$$
\boxed{O(n^2/\varepsilon)\text{ gates},\qquad\text{degree }2\text{ in }n\text{ for fixed }\varepsilon.}
$$
The coarser bound that counts all $\binom n2$ pairs also proves the often-used $O(n^3/\varepsilon)$ construction. Locality improves that cubic estimate to the quadratic bound above; neither assertion is a lower bound on the best possible circuit.

