= Solution
{parent=i}

Use a clean <quantum ancilla> and apply the <quantum circuit> $C$, then the <Pauli Z gate> on the ancilla, then the <inverse quantum circuit> $C^\dagger$:
$$
|x,0\rangle\xrightarrow{C}|x,f(x)\rangle
\xrightarrow{I\otimes Z}(-1)^{f(x)}|x,f(x)\rangle
\xrightarrow{C^\dagger}(-1)^{f(x)}|x,0\rangle.
$$
This <compute-phase-uncompute construction> implements $A\otimes I$ on every input with the ancilla initially $|0\rangle$, and by <linearity> on any superposition of those inputs. The action on ancilla-$|1\rangle$ inputs need not coincide with $A\otimes I$; the question only requires the clean-ancilla subspace.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since $A|x\rangle=(-1)^{f(x)}|x\rangle$, the <spectral decomposition> gives
$$
e^{iA}|x\rangle=e^{i(-1)^{f(x)}}|x\rangle.
$$
Replace the middle gate of the previous <compute-phase-uncompute construction> by
$$
e^{iZ}=\operatorname{diag}(e^i,e^{-i})=R_z(-2),
$$
a one-qubit <rotation about the z-axis>. Then
$$
\boxed{C^\dagger(I\otimes e^{iZ})C\,|x,0\rangle
=e^{i(-1)^{f(x)}}|x,0\rangle.}
$$
The circuit uses the gates of $C$, their inverses, and one fixed rotation; it returns the <quantum ancilla> to $|0\rangle$. The same construction implements $e^{itA}$ with middle gate $e^{itZ}$, an instance of <simulation of a computable diagonal Hamiltonian>.

= 4
